// hooks.proto — what a coordinator needs FROM a platform.
//
// WHY THIS EXISTS
//
// calabi-coord used four RPCs off the control-plane contract: identity's
// ValidateToken and ListEdges, quota's CheckAdmit, metering's ListDeniedOrgs.
// Importing them dragged in the generated control_plane monolith — 11 services,
// ~150 RPCs, every Admin* one — which is what kept the coordinator unpublishable.
//
// This file is the replacement, and it is deliberately NOT a gateway. A gateway
// exists to police a trust boundary; coord runs inside the cluster, so what it
// needed was a narrow CONTRACT, not another hop. These services are registered
// by the SAME servers that already answer coord — identity-svc, quota-svc,
// metering-svc each mount one on its existing port. Zero new deployment units.
//
// The second, better reason to have it: these four calls are the entire surface
// between "a coordinator" and "a platform". A self-hoster who implements them
// plugs coord into their own IdP and their own billing. Contract isolation
// stops being a chore and becomes the integration point.
//
// SHAPE: three services, one per providing server, so each server implements
// exactly what it owns rather than stubbing out its neighbours' methods.
//
// MIRRORING: ValidateToken / CheckAdmit / ListDeniedOrgs restate their
// control-plane originals field-for-field — those messages were already the
// narrow thing you would design here. ListRelayEndpoints does NOT: coord used
// identity's ListEdges purely to build the DERP map, and of EdgeNode's 14
// fields it read four. The other ten (edge_class, edge_class_override,
// reserved_org_id, internal_addr, …) are platform-internal and have no business
// in a public contract, so this asks for what coord actually wants.

